{"id":"015947c7-e55e-4a2f-be50-2056a29643c5","object":"chat.completion","created":1785011795,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n\\n    N = len(word_token_indices)\\n    if len(word_scores) != N:\\n        raise ValueError('Mismatch lengths')\\n    for f in forced_words:\\n        if not (0 <= f < N):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid forced word index')\\n\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[f]) for f in forced_set)\\n    if forced_token_count > token_budget:\\n        raise ValueError('Forced words exceed token budget')\\n\\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_token_count\\n\\n    candidates = []\\n    for i in range(N):\\n        if i not in selected:\\n            candidates.append((word_scores[i], i))\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n\\n    for score, idx in candidates:\\n        word_len = len(word_token_indices[idx])\\n        if word_len <= remaining_budget:\\n            selected.add(idx)\\n            remaining_budget -= word_len\\n\\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    selected_words = []\\n    for i in range(N):\\n        if i in selected:\\n            selected_words.append(i)\\n            retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[i])\\n\\n    tokens_used = len(retained_token_indices)\\n\\n    return {\\n        'retained_token_indices': retained_token_indices,\\n        'selected_words': selected_words,\\n        'tokens_used': tokens_used\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the specification. The task is NLP-LL2-02: \"budgeted_token_reconstruction\". The case payload contains:\n\n- word_token_indices: list of lists of integers, each sublist is the token indices for a word (group of tokens). Tokens are assumed to be consecutive? Not necessarily, but we treat each word as indivisible group.\n- word_scores: list of floats, same length as word_token_indices, representing keep probability/score for each word.\n- forced_words: list of integers (word indices) that must be included.\n- token_budget: integer, maximum number of tokens allowed.\n- _case_nonce: not used.\n\nWe need to select a subset of words (each word is a group of tokens) such that:\n- The forced_words are included.\n- The total number of tokens (sum of lengths of selected word groups) does not exceed token_budget.\n- Among remaining words (non-forced), select them in descending order of word_scores, breaking ties by original word index (source position). That is, we rank all non-forced words by score descending, and for equal score, by original index ascending? The spec says \"Rank remaining words by descending supplied score and then source position.\" It doesn't specify order for ties, but typically ascending source position (lower index first) is a deterministic tie-breaker. Since we want deterministic, we'll do that.\n- We select words greedily: after forced words, iterate through the sorted list of remaining words, and if the word's token group fits within the remaining budget, include it; otherwise skip.\n- Important: The output should include:\n  - retained_token_indices: list of token indices (original token IDs) that are selected, in the order they appear in the original text (i.e., sorted by token index). The spec says \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So we need to return token indices in ascending order (since tokens are assumed to be in order). However, note that word_token_indices might not be sorted? They likely are given sequential. We should flatten all selected word groups and sort the tokens by their index. Or we could just output them in the order they appear as we select words in original word order? The spec says \"return words and tokens in original order\". The word order is the original word order (0 to N-1). So we should process selected words in original word order (by word index) to output retained_token_indices and selected_words. But the selection algorithm is greedy based on ranking, not based on original order. After deciding which words are selected, we need to output the token indices in the order they appear in the original text (i.e., sorted by token index). Because tokens are contiguous across words, if we just concatenate tokens in word order, they will be in increasing token index. So we should iterate through words in original order, collect tokens for selected words. That will produce sorted token indices.\n\n- selected_words: list of word indices (integers) that are selected, also in original order. We'll output the selected word indices in increasing order.\n\n- tokens_used: total number of tokens selected.\n\nAlso, validate: if forced words cannot be included within token budget (sum of their token counts > token_budget), raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation outline:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract fields\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\n    \n    N = len(word_token_indices)\n    # Validate lengths\n    if len(word_scores) != N:\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatch between word_token_indices and word_scores length\")\n    # Validate forced_words indices are valid\n    for f in forced_words:\n        if not (0 <= f < N):\n            raise ValueError(\"Invalid forced word index\")\n    # Compute forced tokens count\n    forced_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[f]) for f in forced_words)\n    if forced_token_count > token_budget:\n        raise ValueError(\"Forced words exceed token budget\")\n    \n    # Mark forced words as selected initially, but we need to consider them in selection order? Actually forced are always selected. \n    selected = set(forced_words)\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_token_count\n    \n    # Build list of non-forced words with (score, index) for ranking\n    candidates = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        if i not in selected: # not forced\n            candidates.append((word_scores[i], i))\n    # Sort descending by score, then ascending index (since source position - lower index first)\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\n    \n    # Greedy selection\n    for score, idx in candidates:\n        word_len = len(word_token_indices[idx])\n        if word_len <= remaining_budget:\n            selected.add(idx)\n            remaining_budget -= word_len\n    \n    # Build output in original order\n    retained_token_indices = []\n    selected_words = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        if i in selected:\n            selected_words.append(i)\n            retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[i])\n    \n    tokens_used = len(retained_token_indices)\n    \n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained_token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. We use only standard library.\n\nNow produce the JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string. Escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWe'll write the source code as a string.\n\nMake sure to use proper indentation.\n\nAlso note: the spec says \"Select forced words first and reject an infeasible forced set.\" So we do that.\n\nEdge cases: forced words may be empty, then it's fine. Also, there might be duplicate forced words? Unlikely but we can handle by set.\n\nWe'll also add a check that forced_words list does not contain duplicates (since duplicate doesn't change selection but may cause double counting token count). We'll just use set, but if there are duplicates, the sum of lengths may count twice. Better to check if len(forced_words) != len(set(forced_words)) and raise? Not specified, but to be safe, we'll treat duplicates as redundant? The spec says \"forced_words list\" so it's a list. Typically it's a set-like but we can handle by using set to avoid double counting. However, if duplicates present, they might intend to force that word multiple times? That doesn't make sense because a word can be selected only once. So we'll deduplicate when counting token cost and selection. But we should output selected_words in original order without duplicates. So use set for selection, but if duplicates are present, we ignore extras. The input likely won't have duplicates. We'll just convert forced_words to a set for selection, but keep the original list for validation? No, we can use set for selection and for counting. We'll compute forced_token_count by summing over unique forced words (set). But then problems if duplicates? We'll assume no duplicates.\n\nAlternatively, we can iterate forced_words and add to selected set, counting each occurrence, but that would count duplicate words multiple times, which is wrong. So we'll deduplicate.\n\nI'll use forced_set = set(forced_words) and compute forced_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[f]) for f in forced_set). Then validate that this count <= budget.\n\nBut if there's a duplicate, we still need to output only one occurrence in selected_words. So dedup is fine.\n\nNow produce the final JSON.\n\nLet me write the code as a string.\n\nNote: In the spec, the output schema has \"tokens_used\" as integer. We compute that.\n\nMake sure the function returns the dict.\n\nNow encode as JSON string.\n\nI'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    \\n    N = len(word_token_indices)\\n    if len(word_scores) != N:\\n        raise ValueError('Mismatch lengths')\\n    for f in forced_words:\\n        if not (0 <= f < N):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid forced word index')\\n    \\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[f]) for f in forced_set)\\n    if forced_token_count > token_budget:\\n        raise ValueError('Forced words exceed token budget')\\n    \\n    selected = set(forced_set)\\n    remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_token_count\\n    \\n    candidates = []\\n    for i in range(N):\\n        if i not in selected:\\n            candidates.append((word_scores[i], i))\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\\n    \\n    for score, idx in candidates:\\n        word_len = len(word_token_indices[idx])\\n        if word_len <= remaining_budget:\\n            selected.add(idx)\\n            remaining_budget -= word_len\\n    \\n    retained_token_indices = []\\n    selected_words = []\\n    for i in range(N):\\n        if i in selected:\\n            selected_words.append(i)\\n            retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[i])\\n    \\n    tokens_used = len(retained_token_indices)\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'retained_token_indices': retained_token_indices,\\n        'selected_words': selected_words,\\n        'tokens_used': tokens_used\\n    }\"}\n\nWe need to escape newlines and quotes. In JSON, we replace newline with \\n, double quotes with \\\", backslash with \\\\.\n\nIn the code we use single quotes for dict keys and string literals, so we don't need to escape double quotes. But we must escape backslashes if any (none here). Also newlines.\n\nWe'll construct the string properly.\n\nAlternatively, we can write it as a raw string in Python and then json.dumps it. But since we are outputting JSON, we need to manually escape. I'll carefully write the string.\n\nI'll write the code, then encode.\n\nLet me write the code line by line, then join with newline.\n\ncode_lines = [\n    \"def solve(case):\",\n    \"    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\",\n    \"    word_scores = case['word_scores']\",\n    \"    forced_words = case['forced_words']\",\n    \"    token_budget = case['token_budget']\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    N = len(word_token_indices)\",\n    \"    if len(word_scores) != N:\",\n    \"        raise ValueError('Mismatch lengths')\",\n    \"    for f in forced_words:\",\n    \"        if not (0 <= f < N):\",\n    \"            raise ValueError('Invalid forced word index')\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    forced_set = set(forced_words)\",\n    \"    forced_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[f]) for f in forced_set)\",\n    \"    if forced_token_count > token_budget:\",\n    \"        raise ValueError('Forced words exceed token budget')\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    selected = set(forced_set)\",\n    \"    remaining_budget = token_budget - forced_token_count\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    candidates = []\",\n    \"    for i in range(N):\",\n    \"        if i not in selected:\",\n    \"            candidates.append((word_scores[i], i))\",\n    \"    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[0], x[1]))\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    for score, idx in candidates:\",\n    \"        word_len = len(word_token_indices[idx])\",\n    \"        if word_len <= remaining_budget:\",\n    \"            selected.add(idx)\",\n    \"            remaining_budget -= word_len\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    retained_token_indices = []\",\n    \"    selected_words = []\",\n    \"    for i in range(N):\",\n    \"        if i in selected:\",\n    \"            selected_words.append(i)\",\n    \"            retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[i])\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    tokens_used = len(retained_token_indices)\",\n    \"\",\n    \"    return {\",\n    \"        'retained_token_indices': retained_token_indices,\",\n    \"        'selected_words': selected_words,\",\n    \"        'tokens_used': tokens_used\",\n    \"    }\"\n]\n\nThen join with '\\n' and escape for JSON.\n\nBut we also need to ensure that the JSON string is properly escaped. We'll do it manually.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON object.\n\nI'll output exactly as required."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1470,"completion_tokens":3207,"total_tokens":4677,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2790},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":62},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}